---
import '../styles/global.css';
import Brand from '../components/Brand';
import record from '../../docs/evidence/2026-10-15-mock-http/audited/walkthrough.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
const labels: Record<string, string> = {
  'response-loss': 'Supplier saves the order, confirmation is lost',
  'repeat-dispatch-after-both-process-restarts': 'Restart and repeat the verified order',
  'changed-payload-held': 'Change the amount under the same order ID',
  'revoked-during-429-wait': 'Revoke approval while waiting to retry',
  'revoked-after-admission-may-commit': 'Revoke after the send has been admitted',
};
if (record.status !== 'passed' || record.vendorReadback.verification !== 'verified')
  throw Error('The walkthrough requires an actual verified local report.');
const source = 'https://github.com/mastodonsharkgirl/field-relay/tree/codex/field-relay-mock-http';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
    <title>Field Relay — interview walkthrough</title>
    <link rel="icon" href={base + 'favicon.svg'} type="image/svg+xml" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="A five-minute walkthrough of CSV reconciliation and a recorded local experiment in uncertain order delivery."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <main class="tour">
      <header class="masthead">
        <Brand href={base} />
        <nav aria-label="Walkthrough navigation">
          <a href={base + 'case-study/'}>Case study</a>
          <a href={source}>Source</a>
        </nav>
      </header>
      <section class="tour-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">INTERVIEW WALKTHROUGH / FICTIONAL ORDERS</p>
        <h1>Walk through Field Relay in five minutes.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Find a difference. Inspect the original rows. Then explain what happens when an order is
          saved but its confirmation never arrives.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is an AI-assisted portfolio prototype. The supply-chain coordinator below is
          hypothetical. The website works with files in your browser; the separate HTTP experiment
          was run locally.
        </p>
        <a class="tour-action" href={base}>Open the reconciliation desk</a>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="compare">
        <p class="eyebrow">01 / TRY THE WORKING DESK</p>
        <h2 id="compare">What changed between these exports?</h2>
        <p>
          Imagine a coordinator comparing a supplier export with the team’s sheet before a handoff.
          Some values have changed. An order is missing. An ID appears twice.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Open the desk. The fictional exports and their field mappings are already loaded. Choose <strong
              >Reload example</strong
            > to start over, or <strong>Try an example</strong> after clearing the example.
          </li>
          <li>
            Select a finding. Read the baseline and candidate rows instead of guessing which version
            is right.
          </li>
          <li>
            Record a review decision with a reason. Duplicate and blank IDs remain ambiguous.
            Download the evidence before closing the tab.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="note">
          “Local” means these files stay in the current browser tab. Reviewing a difference does not
          send an order or update another system.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="delivery">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 / EXPLAIN UNCERTAIN DELIVERY</p>
        <h2 id="delivery">A timeout does not tell you whether the order was saved.</h2>
        <p>
          The coordinator submits an approved order. The supplier saves it, but the reply is lost.
          Sending a new order could create a duplicate. Assuming nothing happened could hide
          accepted work.
        </p>
        <div class="flow" aria-label="Order recovery sequence">
          <span>Approve exact fields</span><span>Send with a stable reference</span><span
            >Reply is lost</span
          ><span>Read the supplier’s saved order</span><span>Verify the exact receipt</span>
        </div>
        <p>
          The local relay preserves decisions through restarts, limits retries and holds conflicting
          evidence for investigation. Approval before a send and revocation after a send are
          different events: an already admitted request can still commit.
        </p>
        <details>
          <summary>What can I actually operate in this website?</summary>
          <p>
            The reconciliation desk and the <strong>Delivery simulator lab</strong> run in the browser.
            The simulator is a separate fictional exercise with outage, retry and replay controls. It
            consumes no reconciliation result. The Python/SQLite HTTP experiment shown below is recorded
            evidence, not a remotely connected backend.
          </p>
        </details>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="proof">
        <p class="eyebrow">03 / CHECK THE EVIDENCE</p>
        <h2 id="proof">Recorded local HTTP experiment</h2>
        <p>
          <strong
            >{record.scenarioResults.length} scenarios passed; {record.vendorReadback.count} exact supplier
            orders verified.</strong
          > These are measured results from fictional local fixtures, including injected failures. They
          are not customer outcomes.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Recorded scenario results">
          <table>
            <thead
              ><tr
                ><th scope="col">Scenario</th><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col"
                  >Verified orders for this scenario</th
                ></tr
              ></thead
            >
            <tbody
              >{
                record.scenarioResults.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <>
                      <th scope="row">{labels[row.name] ?? row.name}</th>
                      <td>{row.status}</td>
                      <td>{row.observedCount ?? 'unverified'}</td>
                    </>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }</tbody
            >
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          Recorded 15 October 2026. Source {record.metadata.sourceSha.slice(0, 7)}; clean source: {
            String(!record.metadata.dirty)
          }. Scenario counts refer to separate checks and must not be added together as unique
          orders.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href={source + '/docs/evidence/2026-10-15-mock-http'}
            >Inspect the reports and failed attempts</a
          >. An earlier run stopped before admission and did not retain its exact cause. The repair
          separates relay startup from the supplier wait and preserves safe failure codes.
          Historical failures remain visible.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="explain">
        <p class="eyebrow">04 / SAY IT IN YOUR OWN WORDS</p>
        <h2 id="explain">The answer you should be able to defend.</h2>
        <blockquote>
          “I built Field Relay with AI coding assistance to explore an operations problem: how do
          you understand changes between exports, and recover when an order request times out? The
          desk shows source rows. The separate local lab keeps the approved order fixed, uses one
          stable identity, and checks what the supplier actually saved. I inject failures and
          inspect the results. It is a learning prototype using fictional data.”
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          Be ready to explain why a successful response is only a claim, why a changed amount needs
          fresh approval, and why bounded retries can leave an unresolved exception. Describe your
          own decisions and learning honestly.
        </p>
        <details>
          <summary>Where could Claude fit later?</summary>
          <p>
            A proposed extension could turn a messy supplier message into an order proposal.
            Application code would validate it, a person would approve exact fields, and the
            deterministic relay would handle delivery. A model should not infer permission or invent
            a delivery status. No Claude call exists in this prototype.
          </p>
        </details>
        <p>
          <a href={source + '/docs/integration/INTERVIEW_GUIDE.md'}
            >Read the complete interview guide</a
          > · <a href={base}>Return to the desk</a>
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
